00:00:32.977 --> 00:00:34.810
YURI GRINSHTEYN: My
name is Yuri Grinshteyn,

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:36.280
and I'm a site
reliability engineer

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.720
at Google Cloud on the customer
reliability engineering team.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.973
So here are the topics
we'll be covering today.

00:00:41.973 --> 00:00:43.640
We'll start by
understanding the basics.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.680
We'll define the
concept of toil as it

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:48.580
relates to the practices of
site reliability engineering.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:50.840
From there, we'll
cover the objectives

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:52.970
of as code practices.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:55.310
Next, we'll review the specific
Google Cloud monitoring

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.620
entities that can be
automated and compared,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.405
three approaches to automating.

00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:01.280
I'll wrap up the first
portion of the session

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.770
by showing you a couple of
specific examples of automating

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.270
Cloud monitoring entities.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:06.770
From there, I'll
hand it over to Pod

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.680
to cover the lessons learned
at Schlumberger, as they

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.390
have pursued this in earnest.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:12.930
Let's get started.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:14.510
So first, what do
we mean by toil

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:17.250
and why is automation important?

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.290
Well if we go back to our
fundamental principles

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.200
and practices, we'll recall that
site reliability engineering,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:25.700
or SRE, is a specific set of
practices developed at Google

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.890
and since embraced everywhere
that hold user happiness

00:01:28.890 --> 00:01:31.170
and appropriate
reliability at scale

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:32.950
as their ultimate objectives.

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:36.630
One of the major areas of focus
for both the more generalized

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.150
DevOps movement and SRE, which
is a specific implementation

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.910
of it, there's the focus
on automating as much

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.210
of the repetitive manual
tasks that specifically don't

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.850
add enduring value as possible.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.490
Ultimately, our objective is to
achieve the appropriate level

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:54.840
of reliability for our services
and maintain that level, even

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:57.330
as user demand and
load increases,

00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:59.970
without having to proportionally
scale the staff required

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.710
to support that service.

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.680
This is really the ultimate
value of focusing automation.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.595
It allows us to reach much
greater levels of scale.

00:02:07.595 --> 00:02:09.470
This is, of course, a
very simplified version

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:10.440
of the answer.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.300
You can find much
more detail on why

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.190
we focus on reducing
toil in the books

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.610
we've published on the topic.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.370
I'll particularly
encourage you to review

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.530
chapter 5 of the
first SRE book called

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.690
"Eliminating Toil,"
which discusses

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.360
this in much greater detail.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.480
So now that we've covered
the value of automation,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.730
let's talk about what we
mean when we say that we want

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:31.315
to treat something as code.

00:02:31.315 --> 00:02:33.440
Here are the things we'll
be looking to accomplish.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.482
Now a lot of this comes
from the infrastructure

00:02:35.482 --> 00:02:37.190
as code principles,
but there's no reason

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:39.320
why we can't think
of monitoring as code

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:41.700
using the same exact language.

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:44.238
So first, we want to be
able to accurately determine

00:02:44.238 --> 00:02:46.280
if there are any differences
between what we want

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.630
to be the key to our
monitoring configuration,

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:50.485
and what is actually
currently the case.

00:02:50.485 --> 00:02:51.860
And so to that
end, we need a way

00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.410
to precisely specify
the desired state

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.590
and be able to retrieve
the current state.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.175
Next, we want our
work to be reusable.

00:03:00.175 --> 00:03:02.300
We want to be able to create
modules and components

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.760
that other folks can use.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:05.510
And again, we're in
pursuit of scale here,

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.090
and that can't happen if every
service team has to build this

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:09.950
from scratch, especially
in a model where

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:13.510
SRE is a shared service
across multiple service teams.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.635
We want to use a declarative
approach to specifying

00:03:15.635 --> 00:03:17.005
our desired state.

00:03:17.005 --> 00:03:18.380
This means that
we want to define

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.630
the state we want,
not the set of actions

00:03:20.630 --> 00:03:21.982
to take to get there.

00:03:21.982 --> 00:03:23.690
Now, one of the benefits
of this approach

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:26.120
is that it's
[INAUDIBLE],, which means

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.460
that we can run our
automation no matter

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.520
what the current state is and
still be sure we'll arrive

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:33.410
at the correct end state.

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:35.480
This is different than
an imperative approach

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.480
where we specify the exact
steps that should be taken.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:40.430
That might work best
for programming but not

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.270
for configuration.

00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:44.000
And finally, in
part because we are

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.562
SREs who value software
engineering practices,

00:03:46.562 --> 00:03:48.770
we want to treat these
configurations in the same way

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.397
we treat other source code.

00:03:50.397 --> 00:03:51.980
One important thing
we want to achieve

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.105
is the other side of
the reusability coin.

00:03:54.105 --> 00:03:56.600
We want to both share
our work and enable

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.040
others to collaborate
and contribute to it.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.470
And this is where
code management

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:02.580
is incredibly important.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.730
So I hope that you're now
convinced of the value

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:08.240
of automation in general and
applying as code principles

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:12.080
to configurations
specifically at this point.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:15.170
The next logical question is
OK, how should we get started?

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.290
What can we actually automate?

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.029
So in Google Cloud
Monitoring, there

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:22.180
are four primary entities
whose creation, updates,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.070
and deletions can be automated.

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:25.570
The first three are
available today,

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.430
and that's where most
of our focus will be.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:29.453
They are uptime checks.

00:04:29.453 --> 00:04:31.120
So this is a feature
of Cloud monitoring

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.320
that allows you to configure
synthetic checks to validate

00:04:35.320 --> 00:04:38.080
things like URL availability,
website performance,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.280
and TCP connectivity on VMs.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:42.380
Alerting policies.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:44.190
So I'll get into their
details in a bit,

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:46.190
but these are how you
configure Cloud monitoring

00:04:46.190 --> 00:04:48.380
to actually send alerts
and notifications when

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.270
specific conditions are met.

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:52.160
And finally, dashboards
are collections

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.770
of charts folks use to
visualize the state of health

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:57.050
of their systems.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:59.300
When looking to automate
Google Cloud Monitoring

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:03.000
configurations, you have
three primary options.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.660
The first option is to use
either the Google Cloud APIs

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.570
directly or make API
calls in your code using

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:11.970
client libraries provided
by Google or third parties.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:14.810
This works very well if you have
a robust software engineering

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.970
practice, and you
need to include

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:20.210
monitoring automation in
larger scripting or cognition

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:22.003
projects.

00:05:22.003 --> 00:05:24.170
Your second option is to
use Google Cloud Deployment

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:25.070
Manager.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:26.870
This is a fully managed
and hosted service

00:05:26.870 --> 00:05:27.780
provided by Google.

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.440
It uses YAML as its
declaration language,

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:33.020
and the current state of your
configuration, once you apply,

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:34.997
is then stored in the Cloud.

00:05:34.997 --> 00:05:37.580
You can interact with Deployment
Manager using gcloud commands

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:39.710
or its own APIs.

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:41.480
And then your third
option, and the one

00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:44.900
I'll be using for our examples
coming up next, is Terraform.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.080
This is an open source
toolkit provided by HashiCorp.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.418
It uses its own language
for declarations.

00:05:50.418 --> 00:05:52.460
But it has an excellent
multicloud support, which

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.185
is important for many users.

00:05:54.185 --> 00:05:55.560
The open source
version, which is

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.310
what I'll be showing you
today, stores state locally

00:05:58.310 --> 00:05:59.902
in dot tfstate files.

00:05:59.902 --> 00:06:01.860
So these can be checked
with the source control

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.740
just like the main
configuration files.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:06.770
So I imagine by now
this is probably

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:08.430
enough theory for everyone.

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:11.665
Let's get into some
specific examples.

00:06:11.665 --> 00:06:13.040
Let's start with
a simple example

00:06:13.040 --> 00:06:14.580
of creating an uptime check.

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:16.080
As you may recall,
uptime checks are

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.110
used to measure what
service or TCP endpoint

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.950
availability [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.860
Here's what our Terraform
file looks like.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:23.730
There are five
things to note here.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:25.803
First, at the very top,
we specify the provider

00:06:25.803 --> 00:06:28.220
to let Terraform know that
we're working with Google Cloud

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.980
and to provide the project
we're operating in.

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:32.670
Next, we define the resource.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:34.370
It's an uptime check.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:38.360
In the http_check section,
we specify the path and port

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:41.340
to connect on and whether
or not to use SSL.

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.340
In the monitored
resource section,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:47.250
we specify that we're checking a
URL rather than a TCP endpoint,

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:49.880
the host we want to connect
to, and the project where

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.300
this uptime check
will be created.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:54.380
And finally, we specify
a content match string

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.510
we want to validate
in the response.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.500
In the terminal, we'll run
the Terraform plan command.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.580
It will validate
our configuration,

00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.160
compare it against the current
state, if there is any,

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.690
and determine what resources
need to be updated.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:10.710
Here, the plan is to
add one new resource,

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:13.820
as you can see in the
output at the bottom.

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.550
Once we've confirmed
that the plan is correct,

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.052
we issue the Terraform
apply command.

00:07:19.052 --> 00:07:20.510
It will once again
provide the plan

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.790
and ask for an
explicit confirmation.

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.220
It will then provide the
specific resources that

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:29.132
were created, changed,
or deleted as a result.

00:07:29.132 --> 00:07:29.840
And there we are.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.120
We can now log into
the Cloud console

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:35.850
and validate that our uptime
check has been created.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:37.610
If we want to clean
up what we did,

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.340
we can issue the
Terraform destroy command.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.560
Similarly to apply, it
will create and validate

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:43.400
the plan of action.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.650
It will then ask us
for a confirmation

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.910
before actually
destroying the resource,

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.580
and then let us
know when it's done.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:52.260
And now we're back
to a clean slate.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:56.672
Let's take a look at another
slightly more complex example.

00:07:56.672 --> 00:07:58.130
Once we've created
an uptime check,

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.280
we may want to get alerts
if and when it fails.

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:03.740
Before we get into the code,
let's review the basics.

00:08:03.740 --> 00:08:06.740
We're going to create an
alerting policy, which combines

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:08.540
both the condition
to be evaluated,

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.000
the trigger notifications,
and the mechanism

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:12.670
to be used for the
notification itself

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.510
or the notification channel.

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:17.650
Our Terraform code needs
to describe both of these.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:20.310
So here's how we would
configure the policy in the UI.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.910
We would use uptime check URL
as the resource and the check

00:08:22.910 --> 00:08:24.790
passed as a metric.

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.590
We would then filter it through
just the check we care about.

00:08:27.590 --> 00:08:29.465
We would use the
count false aggregator

00:08:29.465 --> 00:08:31.340
to count how many of
the locations from which

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:33.830
the check is running
report failures.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.110
Our condition will then
trigger if any failures are

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.195
reported within five minutes.

00:08:38.195 --> 00:08:39.820
Here's how we would
write the Terraform

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:41.380
file to represent that.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.140
Note that we specify the
notification channel first

00:08:44.140 --> 00:08:46.960
with type email and
then email address where

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:48.270
the alerts would go to.

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.630
The output would then get
stored in the variable.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.740
We then define our
actual alert policy

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:55.330
with a condition threshold.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:57.730
There, we provide the metric
filter, the comparison

00:08:57.730 --> 00:09:00.110
operator, and the aggregations.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:03.790
And then finally, we specify
the notification channel

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:07.100
we've created up at the top
using the variable all the way

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.420
at the bottom.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.023
When we run Terraform
plan, we see

00:09:10.023 --> 00:09:11.440
that it's actually
going to create

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:15.445
two resources, the notification
channel and the alerting policy

00:09:15.445 --> 00:09:18.200
that is going to
use that channel.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.620
When we run apply, we
see that two resources

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:23.410
are created, as we expect.

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:25.840
As before, we can confirm
that our alerting policy

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.878
was created in the console.

00:09:27.878 --> 00:09:29.920
We can also confirm that
the correct notification

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:33.880
channel was created and that
our policy is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.500
If we want to undo
what we've done,

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.100
we can again issue the
Terraform destroy command.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:40.980
Note that this time, two
resources are destroyed.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.050
Terraform is aware of
the dependencies involved

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.360
and destroys the
alerting policy first

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.370
and then removes the
notification channel.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.200
I hope this served as
a good introduction

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:52.107
to the practice of
monitoring as code

00:09:52.107 --> 00:09:54.190
and how to use Terraform
to automate and configure

00:09:54.190 --> 00:09:56.590
in Google Cloud monitoring
for your projects.

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.240
At this point, I'll
turn it over to Pod

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:00.340
to talk about how the
SRE team at Schlumberger

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:03.240
put these into a
real world practice.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:04.610
PARDEEP SANDHU: Thank you Yuri.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.175
So I'm going to talk to you
about Schlumberger's monitoring

00:10:07.175 --> 00:10:12.390
as code journey over the past
two years, starting with 2018.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:14.900
At Schlumberger, there
are many solution teams

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:18.620
developing apps and services
for the Delfi ecosystem.

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:21.590
And each one of these teams
was creating their own version

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:23.150
of the monitoring framework.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.810
What I mean is they were
creating dashboards or alerts

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:30.260
or even log-based metrics
in their own silos,

00:10:30.260 --> 00:10:32.230
using different
coding languages,

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:35.440
or in some cases just
configuring through the UI.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.580
In terms of the big
picture, it was obviously

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:41.640
great to see so many teams
taking monitoring so seriously.

00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:44.000
But the problem was,
with this approach,

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:47.060
that very little code
was being reused,

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:50.420
which lent it to being hard
to build any best practices

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:51.710
or learnings.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:55.190
And in most cases, word
of mouth or presentations

00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:57.830
were the only way of showing
what each of the teams

00:10:57.830 --> 00:11:00.140
were doing.

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:04.340
So what changed in 2019?

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:07.460
Well, a new central
SRE team was created,

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:09.990
and it's the team
that I work in today.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.560
The purpose of this team
is to provide governance

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:16.780
and alignment across the various
solution teams in Schlumberger.

00:11:16.780 --> 00:11:19.650
At the beginning of 2019,
we started brainstorming

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.280
of what we wanted to
focus on for that year,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:26.310
and after reading various
books on SRE from Google,

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:29.280
we decided it should
be toil management.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.070
After attending many
presentations and demos

00:11:32.070 --> 00:11:33.720
of each of the
monitoring frameworks

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.910
from each of the
teams, we wanted

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:41.370
to merge the implementation
learnings into one toolkit.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:45.330
So the idea of creating one
toolkit or one framework,

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:48.900
we believe it would allow
us to add new monitoring

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:52.780
capabilities that would
benefit all the teams using it.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:56.130
One example of this was when
Google introduced the service

00:11:56.130 --> 00:11:59.710
level monitoring APIs last year.

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:02.970
We could also ramp up
solution teams much faster

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:06.600
with the use of this toolkit
within their apps or services.

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.030
And by using the one
toolkit, we could

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:10.950
spread the message
of how we wanted

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:16.210
to monitor systems across
Schlumberger more consistently.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:17.070
So the fun part.

00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:19.180
Creating the toolkit.

00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:20.860
So what did we do?

00:12:20.860 --> 00:12:23.770
By taking the best bits of each
of the solutions monitoring

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:27.610
scripts, we created a toolkit to
work with the Google monitoring

00:12:27.610 --> 00:12:28.900
endpoints.

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.460
Key to the design
of the toolkit was

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:32.230
to understand that
most teams already

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:35.290
had monitoring setup,
either through the code

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:36.650
or through the UI.

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:40.750
And we want to take the approach
of backing up and restoring.

00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:42.580
Backing up the
aspects of monitoring

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:46.660
that they care about in the form
of JSON files using [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:46.660 --> 00:12:50.100
and providing the ability
to restore from them.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:52.473
Once we'd created the toolkit,
we needed a way of teams

00:12:52.473 --> 00:12:54.390
to adopt it because
there's no point in having

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.310
a toolkit that no one uses.

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:58.770
So we created a second
git repo, which they

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:00.870
could fork from to get started.

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:05.950
And both of these Git repos
are an image in this slide.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:10.300
Within the git repo, we
provided the necessary pipelines

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:14.520
to take our approach of
backing up and restoring.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.740
We also provided the
monitoring controls

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:20.310
that will need to be in
place for all of our GCPs

00:13:20.310 --> 00:13:22.880
for SOC 2 compliance.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:24.720
And we also included
other examples

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:26.790
of how to monitor
different Google Cloud

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:32.010
technologies such as Pub/Sub,
Google Storage Datastore,

00:13:32.010 --> 00:13:33.790
Google Load Balancer.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.870
And for the teams that
were new to SRE practices,

00:13:36.870 --> 00:13:42.380
we also included examples of
how to set up SLIs and SLOs.

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:45.500
Within the git repo that the
solution teams had forked,

00:13:45.500 --> 00:13:47.930
they could also add their
monitoring configuration

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:50.970
that was specific to their app.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.810
We inner sourced
both of these git

00:13:52.810 --> 00:13:56.310
repos so that teams could
contribute new features,

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.920
fix bugs, or even provide
good examples of monitoring

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:04.150
certain aspects, which the
other teams may benefit from.

00:14:04.150 --> 00:14:07.060
Each of these Git repos are
managed through pull requests,

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:09.520
which our team
approve because we

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.980
have the broad knowledge of how
all the other teams are using

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.730
the toolkit, and
we can give advice

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:18.310
on the best ways of monitoring
certain aspects of Google

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:19.780
Cloud.

00:14:19.780 --> 00:14:21.700
Having everyone on
the same toolkit

00:14:21.700 --> 00:14:24.310
gives us the agility
to be more dynamic

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:27.880
with either new technology
adoption or compliance

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:31.500
requirements.

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:33.650
So what are the key
takeaways from our learning

00:14:33.650 --> 00:14:35.240
of our implementation?

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:37.950
I would ask you to go and
look at the Google git

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:39.620
repo in github.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:42.410
There you would find
examples of how to back up

00:14:42.410 --> 00:14:44.880
your alerts using Python.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.520
And I think this
would give you ideas

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:50.330
of how to add monitoring as
code into your organizations.

00:14:53.153 --> 00:14:54.070
So where are we today?

00:14:57.330 --> 00:14:59.165
We believe that within
your organizations,

00:14:59.165 --> 00:15:02.370
you can start by implementing
the following points that

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.220
are shown in this slide.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:07.010
And within our organization,
by consolidating

00:15:07.010 --> 00:15:10.280
into a single
toolkit or framework,

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:12.380
I'm going to explain
to you how we

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.870
have benefited by doing this.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:15.890
Number one.

00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:19.070
New solution teams are no
longer reinventing the wheel.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.350
The learning curve
is less steep,

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:24.110
and the time spent is
significantly reduced

00:15:24.110 --> 00:15:26.250
implementing monitoring.

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:27.180
Number two.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.890
Monitoring is no
longer an afterthought.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:32.910
The automated deployment
allows the solution teams

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:36.660
to implement monitoring early
in their product lifecycle,

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:40.050
enabling them to have the
insights into the reliability

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:42.690
and performance of the
critical user journeys

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:46.020
and metrics that they've
defined in their projects.

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:49.730
This will also improve the
solutions overall reliability

00:15:49.730 --> 00:15:51.920
at the time of launch.

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:53.060
Number three.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:55.160
Continuity and
continuous improvement

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:57.500
is achieved using
code rather than

00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:00.110
having to update multiple
different documentation

00:16:00.110 --> 00:16:01.460
sources.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.760
And lastly, point number four.

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:07.370
The central support organization
has a consistent interface

00:16:07.370 --> 00:16:10.670
for monitoring,
alerting, and reporting

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.500
across the various
solution teams,

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:15.860
both for operational and
business level needs.

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:22.690
This is an example of our
service level objectives

00:16:22.690 --> 00:16:26.020
compliance dashboard
that we created in-house.

00:16:26.020 --> 00:16:29.320
This gives us a single pane
of glass for both business

00:16:29.320 --> 00:16:32.590
and solution teams on how
well our apps and services

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:34.560
are performing.

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.420
Each of the colored
boxes represents

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:39.060
wherever a solution
or shared service

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:42.420
is either in compliant,
non-compliant,

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:45.990
or possibly even
in feature freeze.

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.570
And with that, I'd like to thank
you for listening to me today.

00:16:49.570 --> 00:16:52.602
And I'll hand it over to Yuri.

00:16:52.602 --> 00:16:54.800
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Pod, thank
you very much for that.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:55.790
It was extremely informative.

00:16:55.790 --> 00:16:57.640
I really appreciate
you being here today.

00:16:57.640 --> 00:16:59.490
And thank you to
everyone for watching.

00:16:59.490 --> 00:17:01.470
We hope that [INAUDIBLE]
found this useful,

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:02.970
and we certainly
would encourage you

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:04.670
to get started with
your own journey

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:06.170
toward monitoring as code.

00:17:06.170 --> 00:17:06.770
Thanks again.

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:08.510
Take care.

